From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/block/pflash_cfi01: one post_load state handler at a time

pflash_post_load() registers postload_update_cb() as a VM change state
handler and keeps it in pfl->vmstate; the callback deletes pfl->vmstate when
it runs. Nothing stops a second load from running before the VM does, and
then two handlers are registered while pfl->vmstate names only the second.
On the next "cont", vm_state_notify() runs the first handler, which deletes
the second one: the list walk (QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE protects only the current
entry) then calls the freed entry, which deletes pfl->vmstate again.

Reproducer: a machine with a writable CFI01 flash (arm virt with
-bios or -drive if=pflash), a snapshot "a", and in the monitor:

    (qemu) stop
    (qemu) loadvm a
    (qemu) loadvm a
    (qemu) cont

The second loadvm leaks the first handler and the cont walks freed memory.
On aarch64 virt (-bios edk2-aarch64-code.fd, a Debian 13 guest) QEMU dies
with SIGBUS at the cont; with this patch the guest runs on. (Tested on
QEMU 11.1 with PANDA's series; the code is the same upstream.) A restore of device state from memory after a
loadvm (as an in-memory snapshot does) hits the same path.

Drop an outstanding handler before registering the next. On the usual path
pfl->vmstate is NULL here and nothing changes.
---
 hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c b/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
index 5b9ddb20b18c1b6e950324dd0fbc1bb87d95f2e2..213c5886a879cf2442c6c2db6f1ea5f977ed85c1 100644
--- a/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
+++ b/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
@@ -1031,6 +1031,15 @@ static int pflash_post_load(void *opaque, int version_id)
     PFlashCFI01 *pfl = opaque;
 
     if (!pfl->ro) {
+        /*
+         * One handler at a time. A second load before the VM runs (a loadvm then an
+         * in-memory device-state restore) used to register a second handler: the first
+         * one to run deletes pfl->vmstate, the other one, while vm_state_notify walks
+         * the list, and the walk then calls freed memory.
+         */
+        if (pfl->vmstate) {
+            qemu_del_vm_change_state_handler(pfl->vmstate);
+        }
         pfl->vmstate = qemu_add_vm_change_state_handler(postload_update_cb,
                                                         pfl);
     }
